// @run
// @log 3
// @log 3
// @log 6
// THE DISCRIMINATING PROBE for `type-param-nested-list-field.vl`, and the reason that
// file's message is wrong rather than merely unhelpful.
//
// Both programs produce the SAME instantiated shape, `{v: i32[][]}`:
//
//   type Box<T> = { v: T[][] }   applied at `Box<i32>`    — emit error
//   type Box<T> = { v: T[] }     applied at `Box<i32[]>`  — this file, runs
//
// So the reject is not about nested arrays, not about the element type, and not about
// the type the application yields. It is about the DEPTH the parameter occupies IN
// THE ALIAS BODY: `gaeApplyFieldTy` (`compiler/emit_base.vl`) substitutes exactly the
// spellings `T` and `T[]`, so a body that writes the parameter at depth 0 or 1
// substitutes at ANY argument — including an argument that is itself an array, which
// is what carries the nesting here — while a body that writes it at depth 2 does not
// substitute at all.
//
// Held fixed while varying: one alias, one parameter, one field, one instantiation,
// one element type. The only thing that moves between the two files is which side of
// the application the `[]` is written on.
type Box<T> = { v: T[] }

const b: Box<i32[]> = { v: [[1, 2], [3], [4, 5, 6]] }

print(b.v.length)
print(b.v[1][0])
print(b.v[2][2])
